WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.871
Picture a café changing its menu.

2
00:00:04.871 --> 00:00:08.687
The owner swaps the price cards on the wall one at a time.

3
00:00:08.867 --> 00:00:16.745
A customer who reads the wall halfway through sees new prices for coffee and old ones for cake, a menu that never existed.

4
00:00:16.900 --> 00:00:20.735
A table kept as a folder of files has the same problem.

5
00:00:20.735 --> 00:00:28.753
A job adding 200 files drops them in one by one, and a report that reads the folder halfway counts part of the job.

6
00:00:28.933 --> 00:00:33.813
A careful owner fills a new board in the back room, and hangs it in one move.

7
00:00:33.813 --> 00:00:35.333
A table can do that too.

8
00:00:35.333 --> 00:00:41.923
Write every new file first, then a new list of the table's files, then move one pointer to the new list.

9
00:00:41.923 --> 00:00:45.472
A reader sees the old table or the new one, never a mix.

10
00:00:45.733 --> 00:00:47.168
But here's the catch.

11
00:00:47.168 --> 00:00:51.064
If the list is one file, every change rewrites all of it.

12
00:00:51.064 --> 00:00:56.804
A table of a million files gets a new million-line list, for a change that adds ten.

13
00:00:56.967 --> 00:01:00.338
That's repainting the whole board to change one price.

14
00:01:00.338 --> 00:01:04.646
And if we keep the old versions too, isn't that a mountain of copies?

15
00:01:04.646 --> 00:01:07.455
What we want is to rewrite only what changed.

16
00:01:07.455 --> 00:01:08.641
Hold that question.

17
00:01:08.833 --> 00:01:14.107
Apache Iceberg is a set of free rules and code for tables made of plain files.

18
00:01:14.107 --> 00:01:17.081
Its trick is to build the board from panels.

19
00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:22.820
A new board reuses the old panels as they are, and adds one new panel for the new cards.

20
00:01:23.067 --> 00:01:25.277
Here's a table after two appends.

21
00:01:25.277 --> 00:01:26.148
From the top.

22
00:01:26.148 --> 00:01:28.962
The pointer says which version is current.

23
00:01:28.962 --> 00:01:31.307
Here, it's a tiny file that says 3.

24
00:01:31.667 --> 00:01:34.072
It points to the table metadata file.

25
00:01:34.072 --> 00:01:36.348
Metadata means data about the data.

26
00:01:36.348 --> 00:01:44.150
This file holds the columns, the rule for grouping files by day, and the list of the table's versions, called snapshots.

27
00:01:44.150 --> 00:01:45.645
One of them is current.

28
00:01:45.833 --> 00:01:51.920
Each snapshot has a manifest list: one row per manifest, with the range of days inside it.

29
00:01:52.133 --> 00:02:01.484
Each manifest has one row per data file: where it is, its day, its row count, and the smallest and largest value of every column.

30
00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:07.360
And at the bottom, the data files: plain Parquet, a common file format for tables.

31
00:02:07.567 --> 00:02:14.891
In café terms, a snapshot is a whole board, a manifest is a panel, and the data files are the price cards.

32
00:02:15.133 --> 00:02:21.802
In the lesson's demo, the table was created, then got three appends of 1,000 payments, three days each.

33
00:02:21.802 --> 00:02:23.162
Now the third append.

34
00:02:23.162 --> 00:02:26.659
When it's done, how many metadata files will there be?

35
00:02:26.659 --> 00:02:29.896
And how many manifests will the new snapshot name?

36
00:02:29.896 --> 00:02:30.932
Pause and guess.

37
00:02:34.133 --> 00:02:34.555
Watch.

38
00:02:34.555 --> 00:02:38.351
It writes three data files, and nobody reads them yet.

39
00:02:38.351 --> 00:02:41.023
Then one new manifest that lists them.

40
00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.227
Then one new manifest list, naming the new manifest plus the two old ones.

41
00:02:46.227 --> 00:02:48.808
Then one new metadata file, version 4.

42
00:02:48.967 --> 00:02:52.216
And last, one move of the pointer, from 3 to 4.

43
00:02:52.433 --> 00:02:56.893
So four metadata files: one for creating the table, one per append.

44
00:02:56.893 --> 00:03:00.022
And the new snapshot names all three manifests.

45
00:03:00.022 --> 00:03:02.352
Two of them are reused, not copied.

46
00:03:02.533 --> 00:03:04.460
That's why snapshots are cheap.

47
00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:07.816
Two snapshots share every manifest that didn't change.

48
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:10.985
Here the café picture breaks, in a useful place.

49
00:03:10.985 --> 00:03:13.038
A café throws its old board away.

50
00:03:13.038 --> 00:03:18.324
Iceberg keeps the old snapshots, and they share most of their cards with the new one.

51
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:25.327
A reader walks the tree from the top: the pointer, the metadata, the current snapshot, and its manifest list.

52
00:03:25.500 --> 00:03:35.550
Then it opens only the manifests whose days can match, and only the files whose smallest and largest values can match.

53
00:03:35.550 --> 00:03:40.831
In the newest manifest, amounts run from 149 to 250,067 cents.

54
00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:46.130
Ask for amounts above that, and all three of its files are skipped without opening one.

55
00:03:46.130 --> 00:03:48.843
And notice: the reader never lists the folder.

56
00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:50.399
That cuts both ways.

57
00:03:50.399 --> 00:03:54.557
Copy a file into the folder by hand, and no reader ever sees it.

58
00:03:54.557 --> 00:03:57.675
It's a card pinned to the wall behind the board.

59
00:03:57.833 --> 00:04:01.816
Delete a file by hand, and every snapshot that lists it breaks.

60
00:04:01.816 --> 00:04:04.471
The snapshot is the table, not the folder.

61
00:04:04.471 --> 00:04:06.557
Change the table through Iceberg.

62
00:04:06.833 --> 00:04:08.471
So the moral of the story.

63
00:04:08.471 --> 00:04:10.992
Files are written once and never edited.

64
00:04:10.992 --> 00:04:13.890
The tree reuses everything below what changed.

65
00:04:13.890 --> 00:04:17.292
And one pointer move makes a new version appear whole.

66
00:04:17.533 --> 00:04:18.672
One quick check.

67
00:04:18.672 --> 00:04:23.368
Someone deletes, by hand, a data file that the first append wrote.

68
00:04:23.368 --> 00:04:24.934
Which snapshots break?

69
00:04:24.934 --> 00:04:26.072
Pause and think.

70
00:04:29.300 --> 00:04:30.042
All three.

71
00:04:30.042 --> 00:04:35.308
Manifest 1 is shared by every snapshot, so every board loses that card.
